# 5. Production Improvements

The sample above is good as a reference, but for true production-scale millions/sec, you should improve it further.

## A. Replace `Instant` in every message
`Instant::now()` can be expensive in the hot path.

Better:
- use a cheaper monotonic clock if available
- batch timestamping
- use TSC (`rdtsc`) with calibration on x86 if acceptable

## B. Avoid histogram mutex contention
The histogram lock can become a bottleneck.

Better:
- per-thread histograms
- merge every second in reporter thread

## C. Use lock-free ring buffers
`crossbeam::channel` is good, but for ultra-low latency:
- use Disruptor-style ring buffers
- or custom SPSC/MPSC bounded queues
- shard queues aggressively

## D. Batch routing
Instead of routing one message at a time:
- read N messages
- route in batches
- reduce synchronization overhead

## E. CPU pinning
Pin threads to cores:
- router threads pinned
- consumers pinned
- producers pinned

This improves cache locality and reduces scheduler jitter.

## F. NUMA awareness
On multi-socket systems:
- keep producer/router/consumer on same NUMA node
- allocate memory local to the node

## G. Zero-copy payloads
If payloads are large:
- avoid copying arrays
- use pooled buffers
- use `bytes::Bytes` or slab allocators

## H. Network I/O integration
If this is a real networked messaging system:
- use `mio`, `tokio-uring`, or DPDK/AF_XDP depending on requirements
- parse ingress in batches
- route without extra copies

