## Latency-oriented design notes

To push this further in a legitimate environment:

### 1. Avoid dynamic allocation on hot path
Already done here via preallocated packet objects.

### 2. Use CPU pinning
Pin producer/consumer threads to dedicated cores.

Linux example:
```cpp
#include <pthread.h>
#include <sched.h>

void pin_thread_to_core(int core_id) {
    cpu_set_t cpuset;
    CPU_ZERO(&cpuset);
    CPU_SET(core_id, &cpuset);
    pthread_setaffinity_np(pthread_self(), sizeof(cpu_set_t), &cpuset);
}
```

### 3. NUMA awareness
Allocate memory on the same NUMA node as the processing core.

### 4. Batch RX/TX
Batching amortizes synchronization costs.

### 5. Minimize parsing
If you only care about a few fields, parse only those bytes.

### 6. Use huge pages where appropriate
Can reduce TLB pressure in some workloads.

### 7. Replace `yield()` with bounded spin + backoff
For very low latency, `yield()` may be too expensive.

