# Lower-allocation variant: callback-based zero-copy processing

If you want even lower memory overhead, the best pattern is often to process each line **before the buffer is reused**, instead of returning an owned `Record`.

That allows borrowing directly from the parser buffer.

Here is a more efficient variant:

```rust
use std::io::{self, BufReader, Read};

pub struct BorrowedRecord<'a> {
    pub fields: Vec<&'a [u8]>,
}

impl<'a> BorrowedRecord<'a> {
    pub fn field(&self, idx: usize) -> Option<&'a str> {
        std::str::from_utf8(*self.fields.get(idx)?).ok()
    }
}

pub struct ZeroCopyParser<R: Read> {
    reader: BufReader<R>,
    buf: Vec<u8>,
    start: usize,
    end: usize,
    eof: bool,
}

impl<R: Read> ZeroCopyParser<R> {
    pub fn with_capacity(reader: R, capacity: usize) -> Self {
        Self {
            reader: BufReader::with_capacity(capacity, reader),
            buf: vec![0; capacity],
            start: 0,
            end: 0,
            eof: false,
        }
    }

    fn refill(&mut self) -> io::Result<()> {
        if self.eof {
            return Ok(());
        }

        if self.start > 0 && self.start < self.end {
            let remaining = self.end - self.start;
            self.buf.copy_within(self.start..self.end, 0);
            self.start = 0;
            self.end = remaining;
        } else if self.start == self.end {
            self.start = 0;
            self.end = 0;
        }

        if self.end == self.buf.len() {
            self.buf.resize(self.buf.len().max(1) * 2, 0);
        }

        let n = self.reader.read(&mut self.buf[self.end..])?;
        if n == 0 {
            self.eof = true;
        } else {
            self.end += n;
        }

        Ok(())
    }

    pub fn next_record<'a>(&'a mut self) -> io::Result<Option<BorrowedRecord<'a>>> {
        loop {
            if let Some(pos) = self.buf[self.start..self.end]
                .iter()
                .position(|&b| b == b'\n')
            {
                let line_end = self.start + pos;
                let mut line = &self.buf[self.start..line_end];

                if let Some(&b'\r') = line.last() {
                    line = &line[..line.len() - 1];
                }

                let fields = split_fields(line);
                self.start = line_end + 1;
                return Ok(Some(BorrowedRecord { fields }));
            }

            if self.eof {
                if self.start < self.end {
                    let mut line = &self.buf[self.start..self.end];
                    if let Some(&b'\r') = line.last() {
                        line = &line[..line.len() - 1];
                    }
                    let fields = split_fields(line);
                    self.start = self.end;
                    return Ok(Some(BorrowedRecord { fields }));
                } else {
                    return Ok(None);
                }
            }

            self.refill()?;
        }
    }
}

fn split_fields(line: &[u8]) -> Vec<&[u8]> {
    let mut fields = Vec::with_capacity(8);
    let mut start = 0;

    for (i, &b) in line.iter().enumerate() {
        if b == b',' {
            fields.push(&line[start..i]);
            start = i + 1;
        }
    }
    fields.push(&line[start..]);
    fields
}
```

### Tradeoff
This approach is more memory-efficient, but the returned record is only valid until the next parser call.

That is often ideal in high-throughput pipelines.

