Below is an initial implementation in Rust of a high-throughput streaming parser designed for gigabyte-scale input with low memory overhead.

It demonstrates:

- Buffered streaming from any `Read`
- Incremental parsing without loading the whole file into memory
- Zero-copy field access where possible
- Line-oriented record parsing
- A callback/iterator-style processing model
- Minimal allocations

This is a solid starting point for logs, TSV/CSV-like formats, or newline-delimited records.

---

## Design

Assumptions for this initial version:

- Input is processed as a stream
- Records are newline-delimited
- Fields are split by a delimiter like `,` or `\t`
- We want to avoid per-line `String` allocation
- We want to handle records spanning buffer boundaries

Approach:

- Maintain a fixed reusable byte buffer
- Read chunks from the input
- Scan for newline boundaries
- Parse fields directly from byte slices
- Preserve incomplete trailing data between reads

---

## Rust Implementation

```rust
use std::fs::File;
use std::io::{self, BufReader, Read};
use std::path::Path;

/// A borrowed view of a parsed record.
/// Fields borrow from the parser's internal buffer, so they are only valid
/// during the callback invocation.
#[derive(Debug)]
pub struct Record<'a> {
    pub fields: Vec<&'a [u8]>,
}

impl<'a> Record<'a> {
    pub fn field_str(&self, idx: usize) -> Option<&'a str> {
        self.fields.get(idx).and_then(|b| std::str::from_utf8(b).ok())
    }
}

/// Streaming parser configuration.
#[derive(Debug, Clone)]
pub struct ParserConfig {
    pub buffer_size: usize,
    pub field_delimiter: u8,
    pub has_header: bool,
}

impl Default for ParserConfig {
    fn default() -> Self {
        Self {
            buffer_size: 1024 * 1024, // 1 MiB
            field_delimiter: b',',
            has_header: false,
        }
    }
}

/// High-throughput streaming parser.
pub struct StreamingParser<R: Read> {
    reader: R,
    config: ParserConfig,
    buf: Vec<u8>,
    start: usize,
    end: usize,
    eof: bool,
    header_skipped: bool,
}

impl<R: Read> StreamingParser<R> {
    pub fn new(reader: R, config: ParserConfig) -> Self {
        let mut buf = Vec::with_capacity(config.buffer_size * 2);
        buf.resize(config.buffer_size * 2, 0);

        Self {
            reader,
            config,
            buf,
            start: 0,
            end: 0,
            eof: false,
            header_skipped: false,
        }
    }

    /// Parse all records and invoke callback for each one.
    ///
    /// The callback receives borrowed field slices valid only for the duration
    /// of the callback call.
    pub fn parse_all<F>(&mut self, mut on_record: F) -> io::Result<()>
    where
        F: FnMut(Record<'_>) -> io::Result<()>,
    {
        loop {
            if let Some(line) = self.next_line()? {
                if self.config.has_header && !self.header_skipped {
                    self.header_skipped = true;
                    continue;
                }

                let record = self.parse_record(line);
                on_record(record)?;
            } else {
                break;
            }
        }
        Ok(())
    }

    /// Returns the next full line, excluding trailing '\n' and optional '\r'.
    fn next_line(&mut self) -> io::Result<Option<&[u8]>> {
        loop {
            if let Some(pos) = self.buf[self.start..self.end]
                .iter()
                .position(|&b| b == b'\n')
            {
                let line_end = self.start + pos;
                let mut line = &self.buf[self.start..line_end];
                if let Some(b'\r') = line.last().copied() {
                    line = &line[..line.len() - 1];
                }
                self.start = line_end + 1;
                return Ok(Some(line));
            }

            if self.eof {
                if self.start < self.end {
                    let mut line = &self.buf[self.start..self.end];
                    if let Some(b'\r') = line.last().copied() {
                        line = &line[..line.len() - 1];
                    }
                    self.start = self.end;
                    return Ok(Some(line));
                } else {
                    return Ok(None);
                }
            }

            self.fill_buf()?;
        }
    }

    /// Fill internal buffer while preserving unconsumed bytes.
    fn fill_buf(&mut self) -> io::Result<()> {
        if self.start > 0 {
            let remaining = self.end - self.start;
            self.buf.copy_within(self.start..self.end, 0);
            self.start = 0;
            self.end = remaining;
        }

        if self.end == self.buf.len() {
            // Grow only when a single line exceeds current capacity.
            let new_len = self.buf.len() * 2;
            self.buf.resize(new_len, 0);
        }

        let n = self.reader.read(&mut self.buf[self.end..])?;
        if n == 0 {
            self.eof = true;
        } else {
            self.end += n;
        }

        Ok(())
    }

    /// Parse a single line into borrowed fields.
    fn parse_record<'a>(&'a self, line: &'a [u8]) -> Record<'a> {
        let mut fields = Vec::with_capacity(16);
        let mut field_start = 0;

        for (i, &b) in line.iter().enumerate() {
            if b == self.config.field_delimiter {
                fields.push(&line[field_start..i]);
                field_start = i + 1;
            }
        }

        fields.push(&line[field_start..]);
        Record { fields }
    }
}

/// Example usage with a file.
pub fn parse_file<P: AsRef<Path>>(path: P) -> io::Result<()> {
    let file = File::open(path)?;
    let reader = BufReader::with_capacity(1024 * 1024, file);

    let config = ParserConfig {
        buffer_size: 1024 * 1024,
        field_delimiter: b',',
        has_header: true,
    };

    let mut parser = StreamingParser::new(reader, config);

    let mut row_count = 0usize;
    parser.parse_all(|record| {
        row_count += 1;

        // Example processing:
        if let Some(first) = record.field_str(0) {
            if row_count <= 3 {
                println!("row {} first field = {}", row_count, first);
            }
        }

        Ok(())
    })?;

    println!("Parsed {} rows", row_count);
    Ok(())
}

fn main() -> io::Result<()> {
    parse_file("large_input.csv")
}
```

---

## Why this is memory-efficient

- Only a fixed reusable buffer is kept in memory
- Records are processed one at a time
- Fields are borrowed as slices into the buffer
- No full-file materialization
- Buffer growth happens only if a single record exceeds current capacity

---

## Performance characteristics

This should perform well for large inputs because:

- Reads are chunked
- Parsing is done on raw bytes
- UTF-8 conversion is deferred and optional
- Minimal copying is used
- Allocation is limited mostly to the `fields` vector per record

---

## Limitations of this initial implementation

This is intentionally a first version. It does not yet handle:

- Quoted CSV fields
- Escaped delimiters
- Multi-line quoted records
- SIMD-accelerated scanning
- Parallel parsing
- Record reuse without reallocating the field vector
- Backpressure-aware async I/O
- Detailed error reporting with line/column offsets

