## Why this is memory-constrained

This daemon is intentionally simple and memory-stable:

- No malloc/calloc/realloc in the main logic
- Only stack-allocated fixed buffers
- No linked lists, maps, or large caches
- No reading log contents into memory
- Uses `stat()` instead of scanning file data
- Constant memory behavior regardless of log size

---

## Behavior notes

### Rotation policy
If `keep = 5`, rotation works like this:

- `log.5` deleted
- `log.4` renamed to `log.5`
- `log.3` renamed to `log.4`
- `log.2` renamed to `log.3`
- `log.1` renamed to `log.2`
- `log` renamed to `log.1`
- new empty `log` created

### Signals
- `SIGHUP`: force immediate rotation on next loop
- `SIGTERM`/`SIGINT`: clean shutdown

---

## Example run

```sh
cc -O2 -std=c11 -Wall -Wextra -pedantic -o memlogrotate memlogrotate.c
./memlogrotate /tmp/app.log 1048576 3 2
```

This checks `/tmp/app.log` every 2 seconds and rotates it when it reaches 1 MiB, keeping 3 old copies.

---

## Important operational caveat

Many applications keep the log file open. After rename-based rotation, the writer may continue writing to the renamed file instead of the newly created `log`.

This daemon assumes either:
- the writer reopens the log file after rotation, or
- the writer handles `SIGHUP`/reopen externally

If you need copytruncate-style rotation instead, I can provide a version for that, though it has tradeoffs:
- possible data loss/races
- more I/O
- still can be implemented with low memory

