## Optional stricter memory enforcement

If you want strict process-level memory controls, you can add resource limits early in `main()`:

```c
#include <sys/resource.h>

static int apply_memory_limits(void) {
    struct rlimit rl;

    rl.rlim_cur = 4 * 1024 * 1024; /* 4 MiB soft */
    rl.rlim_max = 4 * 1024 * 1024; /* 4 MiB hard */
    if (setrlimit(RLIMIT_AS, &rl) < 0) {
        return -1;
    }

    rl.rlim_cur = 4 * 1024 * 1024;
    rl.rlim_max = 4 * 1024 * 1024;
    if (setrlimit(RLIMIT_DATA, &rl) < 0) {
        return -1;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

Then call it near startup:

```c
if (apply_memory_limits() < 0) {
    log_error("failed to apply memory limits", NULL);
    return 1;
}
```

Note:
- `RLIMIT_AS` may affect libc behavior and mappings.
- On some systems, `RLIMIT_DATA` is less useful than expected.
- For very constrained environments, compile statically only if acceptable, since static binaries may be larger.

