## How it works

If `/tmp/app.log` exceeds the configured size:
- `app.log.4 -> app.log.5`
- `app.log.3 -> app.log.4`
- `app.log.2 -> app.log.3`
- `app.log.1 -> app.log.2`
- `app.log -> app.log.1`
- create fresh `app.log`

This uses only small stack buffers for path construction.

---

## Memory-constrained design notes

This is intentionally simple to stay within tight limits:

- No heap allocations required during steady-state operation
- No file contents loaded into memory
- Uses `stat()` instead of scanning file data
- Uses fixed-size path buffers
- Optional `RLIMIT_AS` hard cap via `setrlimit()`

If you want even stricter behavior, you can also:
- compile static-free and strip symbols
- lower stack size with `RLIMIT_STACK`
- disable daemon mode
- reduce polling frequency

---

## Limitations

This is a minimal daemon, so it does **not** include:

- Compression of rotated logs
- Time-based rotation
- Config file parsing
- Multiple log files
- inotify support
- PID file / lock file
- syslog/error logging after daemonization
- copytruncate semantics

Also note:
- If another process keeps the old file descriptor open, it may continue writing to the renamed file (`app.log.1`) after rotation. This is standard rename-based rotation behavior.

