## Why this is memory-constrained

This daemon avoids unbounded memory growth by:

- using only stack buffers of fixed size
- not storing file lists
- processing directory entries one at a time
- avoiding malloc/calloc/realloc
- using a fixed polling loop

Memory footprint is therefore small and predictable.

---

## Important limitations

This is minimal, so it does **not** include:

- config file support
- compression
- inode tracking
- copytruncate mode
- locking
- daemonization (`fork`, pidfile, syslog)
- inotify
- per-file policies
- secure handling of hostile paths/symlinks
- reopening file descriptors in other processes

One practical issue: if another process keeps the old file descriptor open, after `rename()` it may continue writing to `app.log.1` instead of the newly created `app.log`. Real systems often signal the writer to reopen logs.

