# Example client usage

You can interact with the daemon over the Unix socket.

## Ping

```bash
printf '{"cmd":"ping"}\n' | socat - UNIX-CONNECT:/tmp/resourced.sock
```

## Get metrics

```bash
printf '{"cmd":"get_metrics"}\n' | socat - UNIX-CONNECT:/tmp/resourced.sock
```

## Get alerts

```bash
printf '{"cmd":"get_alerts"}\n' | socat - UNIX-CONNECT:/tmp/resourced.sock
```

## Update config

```bash
cat <<'EOF' | socat - UNIX-CONNECT:/tmp/resourced.sock
{"cmd":"set_config","config":{
  "sample_interval_ms":1000,
  "control_socket":"/tmp/resourced.sock",
  "thresholds":{
    "cpu_percent_alert":85.0,
    "memory_percent_alert":90.0,
    "disk_percent_alert":95.0,
    "load1_alert":4.0
  },
  "actions":{
    "enable_drop_caches":false,
    "enable_signal_process":true,
    "signal_pid":1234,
    "signal_name":"SIGUSR1"
  }
}}
EOF
```

