Below is a minimal working example of an embedded-style sensor-processing pipeline for accelerometer anomaly detection.

It includes:

- simulated accelerometer input
- basic preprocessing
- feature extraction
- simple anomaly detection
- a loop that mimics embedded firmware behavior

I’ll provide it in C, keeping it small and practical for microcontroller adaptation.

---

## Overview

Pipeline:

1. Read accelerometer sample `(ax, ay, az)`
2. Compute magnitude
3. Apply simple low-pass smoothing
4. Compare against a learned baseline
5. Flag anomaly if deviation exceeds threshold

This is intentionally minimal but functional.

---

## Minimal C Example

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <math.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <stdbool.h>

#define SAMPLE_COUNT 200
#define TRAINING_SAMPLES 50
#define ALPHA 0.2f
#define THRESHOLD 2.5f

typedef struct {
    float ax;
    float ay;
    float az;
} AccelSample;

typedef struct {
    float mean;
    float stddev;
} BaselineModel;

// Simulated accelerometer stream
void generate_sample(int t, AccelSample *s) {
    // Normal motion around 1g on Z axis
    s->ax = 0.02f * sinf(0.1f * t);
    s->ay = 0.02f * cosf(0.1f * t);
    s->az = 1.0f + 0.03f * sinf(0.05f * t);

    // Inject anomaly
    if (t == 120 || t == 121 || t == 122) {
        s->ax += 1.5f;
        s->ay += 1.2f;
        s->az += 0.8f;
    }
}

float magnitude(AccelSample s) {
    return sqrtf(s.ax * s.ax + s.ay * s.ay + s.az * s.az);
}

float lowpass_filter(float input, float prev_output) {
    return ALPHA * input + (1.0f - ALPHA) * prev_output;
}

void train_baseline(float *features, int n, BaselineModel *model) {
    float sum = 0.0f;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        sum += features[i];
    }
    model->mean = sum / n;

    float var = 0.0f;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        float d = features[i] - model->mean;
        var += d * d;
    }
    var /= n;
    model->stddev = sqrtf(var);

    // Avoid divide-by-zero
    if (model->stddev < 1e-6f) {
        model->stddev = 1e-6f;
    }
}

bool is_anomaly(float feature, BaselineModel model) {
    float zscore = fabsf(feature - model.mean) / model.stddev;
    return zscore > THRESHOLD;
}

int main(void) {
    AccelSample s;
    float filtered_mag = 0.0f;
    float training_features[TRAINING_SAMPLES];
    BaselineModel model;

    printf("Collecting training data...\n");

    // Training phase
    for (int t = 0; t < TRAINING_SAMPLES; t++) {
        generate_sample(t, &s);
        float mag = magnitude(s);
        filtered_mag = lowpass_filter(mag, filtered_mag);
        training_features[t] = filtered_mag;
        printf("Train t=%d feature=%.4f\n", t, filtered_mag);
    }

    train_baseline(training_features, TRAINING_SAMPLES, &model);
    printf("\nBaseline trained: mean=%.4f stddev=%.4f\n\n", model.mean, model.stddev);

    // Detection phase
    for (int t = TRAINING_SAMPLES; t < SAMPLE_COUNT; t++) {
        generate_sample(t, &s);
        float mag = magnitude(s);
        filtered_mag = lowpass_filter(mag, filtered_mag);

        bool anomaly = is_anomaly(filtered_mag, model);

        printf("t=%d ax=%.3f ay=%.3f az=%.3f feature=%.4f %s\n",
               t, s.ax, s.ay, s.az, filtered_mag,
               anomaly ? "<-- ANOMALY" : "");
    }

    return 0;
}
```

---

## How it works

### 1. Sensor input
`generate_sample()` simulates accelerometer readings. In real firmware, you would replace this with I2C/SPI sensor reads.

### 2. Feature extraction
We use acceleration magnitude:

```c
sqrt(ax^2 + ay^2 + az^2)
```

This avoids dependence on device orientation.

### 3. Filtering
A first-order low-pass filter smooths noise:

```c
y[n] = alpha * x[n] + (1 - alpha) * y[n-1]
```

### 4. Baseline training
The first `TRAINING_SAMPLES` are assumed normal. We compute:

- mean
- standard deviation

### 5. Anomaly detection
We compute a z-score:

```c
abs(feature - mean) / stddev
```

If it exceeds `THRESHOLD`, we flag an anomaly.

