## Overview

Pipeline:

1. Read accelerometer sample `(x, y, z)`
2. Compute magnitude
3. Smooth with moving average
4. Compare current magnitude to baseline
5. Flag anomaly if deviation exceeds threshold

This is intentionally simple and lightweight.

---

## Minimal C Example

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <math.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

#define WINDOW_SIZE 8
#define THRESHOLD 2.5f
#define NUM_SAMPLES 40

typedef struct {
    float x;
    float y;
    float z;
} AccelSample;

typedef struct {
    float buffer[WINDOW_SIZE];
    int index;
    int count;
    float sum;
} MovingAverage;

void ma_init(MovingAverage *ma) {
    ma->index = 0;
    ma->count = 0;
    ma->sum = 0.0f;
    for (int i = 0; i < WINDOW_SIZE; i++) {
        ma->buffer[i] = 0.0f;
    }
}

float ma_update(MovingAverage *ma, float value) {
    if (ma->count < WINDOW_SIZE) {
        ma->buffer[ma->index] = value;
        ma->sum += value;
        ma->count++;
    } else {
        ma->sum -= ma->buffer[ma->index];
        ma->buffer[ma->index] = value;
        ma->sum += value;
    }

    ma->index = (ma->index + 1) % WINDOW_SIZE;
    return ma->sum / ma->count;
}

float magnitude(AccelSample s) {
    return sqrtf(s.x * s.x + s.y * s.y + s.z * s.z);
}

int detect_anomaly(float current, float baseline, float threshold) {
    float deviation = fabsf(current - baseline);
    return deviation > threshold;
}

void generate_samples(AccelSample *samples, int n) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        samples[i].x = 0.1f * sinf(i * 0.2f);
        samples[i].y = 0.1f * cosf(i * 0.2f);
        samples[i].z = 1.0f + 0.05f * sinf(i * 0.1f);
    }

    // Inject anomaly
    samples[25].x = 3.0f;
    samples[25].y = 2.5f;
    samples[25].z = 4.0f;
}

int main(void) {
    AccelSample samples[NUM_SAMPLES];
    MovingAverage ma;

    generate_samples(samples, NUM_SAMPLES);
    ma_init(&ma);

    printf("idx,x,y,z,mag,baseline,anomaly\n");

    for (int i = 0; i < NUM_SAMPLES; i++) {
        float mag = magnitude(samples[i]);
        float baseline = ma_update(&ma, mag);
        int anomaly = detect_anomaly(mag, baseline, THRESHOLD);

        printf("%d,%.3f,%.3f,%.3f,%.3f,%.3f,%d\n",
               i,
               samples[i].x,
               samples[i].y,
               samples[i].z,
               mag,
               baseline,
               anomaly);
    }

    return 0;
}
```

---

## How it works

### 1. Magnitude
Instead of analyzing `x`, `y`, and `z` separately, we reduce them to:

```c
sqrt(x² + y² + z²)
```

This gives a simple motion intensity measure.

### 2. Moving average baseline
A short rolling average estimates “normal” behavior.

### 3. Anomaly detection
If:

```c
abs(current_magnitude - baseline) > THRESHOLD
```

then the sample is flagged as anomalous.

