## Why this works well in real time

### 1. Fixed timestep
Physics is updated using:

```js
const FIXED_DT = 1 / 120;
```

This means simulation remains stable and deterministic regardless of rendering speed.

### 2. Accumulator loop
Instead of simulating once per rendered frame, we accumulate elapsed time:

```js
accumulator += frameTime;
while (accumulator >= FIXED_DT) {
  world.step(FIXED_DT);
  accumulator -= FIXED_DT;
}
```

This is the standard way to make physics **frame-rate independent**.

### 3. Frame-rate constraint
Rendering is capped to a target FPS:

```js
const TARGET_FPS = 60;
if (now - lastRenderTime < FRAME_DURATION) return;
```

This reduces unnecessary rendering work and helps performance.

### 4. Spiral-of-death prevention
We clamp frame time:

```js
if (frameTime > MAX_FRAME_TIME) frameTime = MAX_FRAME_TIME;
```

This prevents the simulation from trying to process too many updates after a lag spike.

