## How the frame-rate constraint works

This is the important part for real-time simulation stability:

### 1. Fixed timestep
Physics always updates using:

```cpp
const float fixedDt = 1.0f / 120.0f;
```

This keeps the simulation deterministic and more stable than using variable `dt`.

### 2. Frame-time clamp
If rendering stalls or the window is dragged, `elapsed` can become huge. That can explode the simulation.

So we clamp it:

```cpp
elapsed = std::min(elapsed, (double)maxFrameTime);
```

### 3. Accumulator
We accumulate real elapsed time and run physics in fixed chunks:

```cpp
accumulator += elapsed;
while (accumulator >= fixedDt) {
    ...
    accumulator -= fixedDt;
}
```

This decouples rendering from simulation.

