## Why this works well for frame-rate constraints

### 1. Fixed timestep
Physics always runs at:

```cpp
FIXED_DT = 1.0f / 60.0f;
```

This avoids instability from fluctuating render frame times.

### 2. Accumulator loop
If rendering is slow, the simulation catches up:

```cpp
accumulator += frameTime;
while (accumulator >= FIXED_DT) {
    world.step(FIXED_DT);
    accumulator -= FIXED_DT;
}
```

### 3. Frame-time clamp
If a frame stalls badly, we cap the time added:

```cpp
float frameTime = std::min(elapsed.count(), MAX_FRAME_TIME);
```

This prevents one hitch from forcing hundreds of physics steps.

### 4. Substep cap
We also limit the number of physics steps per render frame:

```cpp
substeps < MAX_SUBSTEPS
```

This protects real-time responsiveness.

