## Why this works

### 1. Fixed timestep physics
Physics is updated at a constant rate:

```cpp
const double fixedTimeStep = 1.0 / 120.0;
```

This makes simulation more stable than using variable frame delta directly.

---

### 2. Accumulator pattern
This part:

```cpp
accumulator += frameTime;
while (accumulator >= fixedTimeStep) {
    world.step(fixedTimeStep);
    accumulator -= fixedTimeStep;
}
```

ensures physics stays deterministic and consistent even if rendering frame times vary.

---

### 3. Frame-rate constraint
This part caps rendering/update loop speed:

```cpp
if (workTime < targetFrameDuration) {
    std::this_thread::sleep_for(targetFrameDuration - workTime);
}
```

So the app targets about **60 FPS**.

---

### 4. Spiral-of-death prevention
This line:

```cpp
double frameTime = std::min(elapsed.count(), maxFrameTime);
```

prevents giant frame delays from causing too many physics steps in one frame.

