# How the frame-rate constraint works

This is the important real-time structure:

## 1. Fixed timestep
Instead of simulating with variable `dt`, we always use:

```js
const FIXED_DT = 1 / 120;
```

This improves stability for rigid body dynamics.

## 2. Accumulator
We collect elapsed real time:

```js
accumulator += frameTime;
```

Then step physics in fixed chunks:

```js
while (accumulator >= FIXED_DT) {
  world.step(FIXED_DT);
  accumulator -= FIXED_DT;
}
```

## 3. Frame-time clamp
If a frame stalls badly, `frameTime` can become huge. That would cause too many physics steps and instability. So we clamp it:

```js
if (frameTime > MAX_FRAME_TIME) {
  frameTime = MAX_FRAME_TIME;
}
```

## 4. Maximum physics steps per frame
To keep rendering responsive:

```js
const MAX_STEPS = 5;
```

If the simulation falls behind, we stop after a few steps and discard the rest:

```js
if (steps === MAX_STEPS) {
  accumulator = 0;
}
```

This is a practical frame-rate constraint often used in games.

