## How it works

### 1. Fixed timestep
The simulation runs physics at a constant timestep:

```cpp
const float fixedDt = 1.0f / 120.0f;
```

This improves numerical stability compared to using variable frame time directly.

### 2. Accumulator pattern
Real elapsed time is accumulated, and physics steps are processed as needed:

```cpp
accumulator += frameTime;
while (accumulator >= fixedDt) {
    world.step(fixedDt);
    accumulator -= fixedDt;
}
```

This is a standard real-time simulation technique.

### 3. Frame-rate constraint
The loop tries to maintain about 60 FPS:

```cpp
const float targetFrameTime = 1.0f / 60.0f;
sleep_for(...)
```

### 4. Rigid body integration
The body uses:
- force -> acceleration
- torque -> angular acceleration
- semi-implicit Euler integration

