# 16. Example Azure AD app registration expectations

For auth to work, your API app registration should expose scopes like:

- `api.read`
- `api.write`

And clients calling the API must request those scopes.

For daemon/service-to-service auth, app roles are often better than user scopes. In many enterprise backends, you’ll want policies checking:

- `roles` claim for app permissions
- `scp` claim for delegated user permissions

Example flexible policy:

```csharp
builder.Services.AddAuthorization(options =>
{
    options.AddPolicy("Api.Read", policy =>
        policy.RequireAssertion(context =>
            context.User.HasClaim(c => c.Type == "scp" && c.Value.Split(' ').Contains("api.read")) ||
            context.User.HasClaim(c => c.Type == "roles" && c.Value == "Api.Read")));

    options.AddPolicy("Api.Write", policy =>
        policy.RequireAssertion(context =>
            context.User.HasClaim(c => c.Type == "scp" && c.Value.Split(' ').Contains("api.write")) ||
            context.User.HasClaim(c => c.Type == "roles" && c.Value == "Api.Write")));
});
```

This is often more realistic for enterprise Azure integrations.

